{#
  HERO — Figma 184:13327. Content and geometry both come from _data/hero.js.

  TWO BANDS. The intro (white) and the board (#F2F2F7) are separate full-bleed
  rows; the colour change at their seam is the design, not a gap.

  THE FOLDER IS FIVE SEPARATE ELEMENTS, never one flattened image:

      __back      orange sheet behind everything
      __cards     three <article>s, z-ordered 1 → 3
      __front     yellow flap, painted OVER the lower half of the cards

  That stack is what makes the cards read as tucked INTO the folder, and it is
  the structure the open/drag phase animates. Flattening it to a single export
  would look identical at rest and be impossible to animate afterwards.

  The board is a fixed 1272 × 753 frame in Figma. Everything inside is
  positioned in PERCENT of that frame (see hero.js), and aspect-ratio holds the
  proportions, so the whole composition scales as one piece.

  TWO POSITION SETS PER BOARD CHILD. Each icon, decor image and caption writes
  its desktop custom properties AND a parallel -m set from hero.js's `m` values;
  the <=480 block in _hero.css decides which one each rule reads. It has to work
  this way round: these are INLINE custom properties, and no stylesheet rule can
  override an inline one — but CSS can choose which property to consume. The
  cards need no -m set at all; their percentages are identical in both comps.

  Cards are positioned by their CENTRE and pulled back with translate(-50%,-50%)
  before rotating, so each fans around its own middle. The --tuck-* custom
  properties are identity here and are the seam the open/close animation will
  drive; leaving them in place now means that phase touches no markup.
#}

<section class="hero" id="hero" data-header-theme="light">
  <div class="hero__intro">
    <h1 class="hero__title">
      <span class="hero__line">
        <span class="hero__greeting">{{ hero.title.greeting }}</span>
        <span class="hero__name">{{ hero.title.name }}</span>
      </span>
      {#
        "DESIGNER" IS ARTWORK, NOT TEXT. 184:13342 draws it by hand; there is no
        webfont for that, so the word is the exported drawing (259 x 59, the same
        1:1 export as the comp's 258-wide node) inlined so it can take the H1's
        colour through currentColor. An <img> could not — SVG loaded that way is
        an isolated document CSS never reaches.

        THE WORD IS STILL IN THE DOM. The drawing is aria-hidden and a
        visually-hidden span carries the real text, so the heading's accessible
        name and its indexable content are both still "I'm Pari. Product
        Designer". Losing that to make the word prettier would be a bad trade.
      #}
      <span class="hero__line">
        <span class="hero__role">{{ hero.title.roleLead }}</span>
        <span class="hero__role-outline">
          <span class="hero__role-art" aria-hidden="true"
            >{{ "Assets/images/designer-word.svg" | inlineSvg | safe }}</span
          ><span class="hero__role-text">{{ hero.title.roleOutline }}</span>
        </span>
      </span>
    </h1>
    <p class="hero__tagline">{{ hero.tagline }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="hero__board">
    {%- for icon in hero.icons %}
      <img
        class="hero__icon hero__icon--{{ icon.slug }}"
        src="{{ hero.assetPath }}/{{ icon.file }}"
        alt="{{ icon.label }}"
        {% if not icon.label %}aria-hidden="true"{% endif %}
        draggable="false"
        style="--icon-x: {{ icon.x }}%; --icon-y: {{ icon.y }}%; --icon-size: {{ icon.size }}%; --icon-x-m: {{ icon.m.x }}%; --icon-y-m: {{ icon.m.y }}%; --icon-size-m: {{ icon.m.size }}%"
        data-hero-icon
      />
    {%- endfor %}

    {#
      STATIC DECORATION. Nothing here wiggles, drags or animates — hero-icons.js
      binds only [data-hero-icon], so these are invisible to it. They are pure
      artwork: the drawn arrow and the two product mockups. There is no cursor
      mark here — the board's live CSS cursor is the comp's arrow, and a static
      copy beside it would be a frozen duplicate (see hero.js).
      THE MOCKUPS ARE alt="" + aria-hidden; THE ARROW IS NOT. It used to be —
      back when arrow-pull.svg was the wordless curve and the sentence beside it
      was live HTML that carried the meaning. The re-export bakes the words in as
      outlines, so the graphic IS the sentence now and `alt` is the only thing
      left announcing it. Any decor entry with an `alt` is treated as meaningful;
      the rest stay decorative.
    #}
    {%- for d in hero.decor %}
      <img
        class="hero__decor hero__decor--{{ d.slug }}"
        src="{{ hero.assetPath }}/{{ d.file }}"
        alt="{{ d.alt if d.alt else '' }}"
        {%- if not d.alt %} aria-hidden="true"{% endif %}
        draggable="false"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        style="--decor-x: {{ d.x }}%; --decor-y: {{ d.y }}%; --decor-w: {{ d.w }}%; --decor-x-m: {{ d.m.x }}%; --decor-y-m: {{ d.m.y }}%; --decor-w-m: {{ d.m.w }}%"
      />
    {%- endfor %}

    {#
      THE MOCKUP FILENAMES (450:37859, 392:14769). The comp writes each mockup's
      source filename under it. They were skipped once as Figma annotations —
      they are not; they are text nodes in the board and they render.

      LIVE TEXT, NOT BAKED INTO THE PNGs. Keeping them out of the artwork means
      they stay crisp at every board size, cost nothing to correct, and leave
      the mockup exports untouched.

      aria-hidden, like the mockups they sit under: the name of an asset file is
      not something a screen-reader user is missing.
    #}
    {%- for c in hero.captions %}
      <p
        class="hero__caption hero__caption--{{ c.slug }}"
        aria-hidden="true"
        style="--caption-x: {{ c.x }}%; --caption-y: {{ c.y }}%; --caption-w: {{ c.w }}%; --caption-x-m: {{ c.m.x }}%; --caption-y-m: {{ c.m.y }}%; --caption-w-m: {{ c.m.w }}%"
      >{{ c.text }}</p>
    {%- endfor %}

    {#
      NO <p> FOR THE ARROW'S WORDS ANY MORE. They were live HTML in Caveat while
      arrow-pull.svg was the wordless curve; 465:61243 now exports as one graphic
      with the letterforms outlined, so the words are in the artwork above and
      this element would draw them a second time on top. Its accessible text
      lives on that <img>'s alt.
    #}

    {#
      THE FLAP IS THE BUTTON. It cannot wrap the whole folder: the cards are
      draggable, and a <button> containing them would nest interactive controls
      AND make every pointerdown-to-drag fight the button's click. The flap is
      already the topmost layer and already sits exactly over the folder art, so
      it is the natural grab target — and the cards stay siblings, never
      descendants, so they remain independently draggable.

      The markup carries NO state class. Rest — cards peeking, as the comp draws
      them — is what plain CSS produces with no custom properties set;
      hero-folder.js only ever ADDS .is-expanded. So with JS absent or slow, the
      hero still renders correctly and the cards never disappear.
    #}
    <div class="hero-folder" data-hero-folder>
      <img
        class="hero-folder__back"
        src="{{ hero.assetPath }}/{{ hero.folder.back.file }}"
        alt=""
        aria-hidden="true"
      />

      <div class="hero-folder__cards" id="hero-folder-cards">
        {%- for card in hero.cards %}
          <article
            class="hero-folder__card hero-folder__card--{{ loop.index }}"
            style="--card-x: {{ card.x }}%; --card-y: {{ card.y }}%; --card-rot: {{ card.rotate }}deg"
          >
            <h2 class="hero-folder__card-title">{{ card.title }}</h2>
            {#- Every card's body is two toned spans in Figma — an opening
                sentence in ink, the remainder muted. A plain string is still
                accepted so a future single-tone card needs no new branch. -#}
            <p class="hero-folder__card-body">
              {%- if card.body is string -%}
                {{ card.body }}
              {%- else -%}
                {%- for part in card.body -%}
                  <span class="hero-folder__card-body--{{ part.tone }}">{{ part.text }}</span>
                {%- endfor -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            </p>
          </article>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>

      <button
        type="button"
        class="hero-folder__front"
        aria-expanded="false"
        aria-controls="hero-folder-cards"
      >
        <img
          src="{{ hero.assetPath }}/{{ hero.folder.front.file }}"
          alt=""
          aria-hidden="true"
          draggable="false"
        />
        {#
          TWO SPANS, AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.

          [data-folder-label] stays visually hidden and stays the BUTTON'S
          ACCESSIBLE NAME — hero-folder.js swaps it between "Pull the cards out"
          and "Put the cards back" on every toggle, so it has to describe the
          action, not the section.

          The visible pill is "My Expertise" (432:11655), which never changes.
          Putting the two on one element would have the toggle relabel the
          artwork to "Put the cards back" the first time it is clicked. It is
          aria-hidden because the button already has its name from the span
          above, and it sits INSIDE the button so the pill is part of the
          toggle's hit area rather than a dead patch in the middle of it.
        #}
        <span class="hero-folder__label" data-folder-label>Pull the cards out</span>
        <span
          class="hero-folder__expertise"
          aria-hidden="true"
          style="--label-x: {{ hero.expertisePos.x }}%; --label-y: {{ hero.expertisePos.y }}%"
        >{{ hero.expertise.label }}</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {#
    THE BOARD'S RUNWAY. .hero__board is sticky; this empty block is what gives it
    somewhere to be sticky FOR. A sticky box holds until its containing block's
    content runs out, so without a sibling after it the board would meet the
    header and release in the same frame.

    A REAL ELEMENT, NOT .hero::after — that pseudo-element and its ::before pair
    are already the hero's gutter rules (the two vertical strokes at x=120), and
    they are absolutely positioned, so anything given to them takes no space in
    flow and buys no sticky range at all.

    aria-hidden and empty: it is scroll distance, not content.
  #}
  <div class="hero__runway" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</section>
